// THE SHARED BUTTON, flat. It was a copy of MUI's stock outlined button —
// uppercase, a 0.0892857143em tracking nobody chose, a near-black 87% border,
// and a box-shadow in the transition list — which is the look the rest of the
// site has been moving off. The paint now comes from the prose-form tokens
// (app/styles/prose-form.styl, injected into every stylesheet), so a button
// sits in the same ink and the same rules as every control around it.
//
// FLAT MEANS NO SHADOW AND NO RAISE, in the resting, hover and active states
// alike. A pressed button answers with a fill, not with a drop shadow and not
// with a transform — the two things that put a control back on a fake z-axis.
//
// Every hook the Button component can actually produce is preserved: __small,
// __text, __active and the .count badge.
//
// THREE VARIANTS WERE DROPPED, and one of them is why the others were suspect.
// `__failure` painted its ground with `$failureColor`, which is defined in no
// stylesheet in this repo — stylus emits an undefined variable as a bare ident,
// so `background-color: failureColor` was invalid CSS the browser discarded and
// the variant had never once rendered a red button. Nothing in `app/` sets that
// class, or `__success`, or `__outline`. All three were removed rather than
// repaired: a token invented for a variant with no call site is a guess about a
// design decision nobody has made.
//
// `__icon` STAYS, and the reason is worth stating because it is the opposite of
// what it looks like. `icon-button.js` renders OUR `Button` with
// `className='button__icon …'`, and Button prepends `button` to whatever
// className it is handed — so an icon button carries BOTH classes and every
// rule in this file lands on it. Thirteen call sites sit in player rows, the
// roster, the draft board and the auction, so the light fill and the hover lift
// below would have put a grey block and a drop shadow behind each of them.
//
// The :not() chain keeps this off MUI's own buttons, which carry .button at
// several call sites and bring their own paint.
.button:not(.MuiButton-root):not(.MuiIconButton-root):not(.MuiFab-root):not(.MuiButtonBase-root)
  position relative
  display inline-flex
  align-items center
  justify-content center
  // The space between a glyph and its label. MUI's startIcon and endIcon each
  // carried their own 8px margin; the call sites that used them now pass the
  // Icon as an ordinary child, so the gap belongs to the button. `gap` applies
  // only BETWEEN flex children, so this is inert on the icon buttons (one
  // child) and on `.count`, which is out of flow.
  gap 8px
  // $control_height, and box-sizing with it, so this lines up EXACTLY with a
  // text input beside it. The border is inside the box, which is what lets the
  // border go from transparent to visible on hover without the button growing.
  box-sizing border-box
  height $control_height
  min-width 30px
  padding 0 16px
  vertical-align middle
  text-align center
  font-family $prose_sans
  font-size 0.875rem
  font-weight 500
  // No tracking and no uppercase. Both were MUI's, and both are what made a
  // button read as a different typeface from the label beside it.
  letter-spacing 0
  color $prose_ink
  // A LIGHT FILL RATHER THAN AN OUTLINE. The button used to be defined by its
  // edge — a 1px near-black rule around nothing — which is the outlined-button
  // idiom and the heaviest way to draw a control that is rarely the point of
  // its surface. The fill carries the shape now and the border is invisible at
  // rest, so a row of buttons reads as a row of soft blocks rather than as a
  // row of boxes.
  background $control_fill
  background-image none
  // Transparent rather than absent: it holds the 1px in the box model, so the
  // hover state can colour it in without the button changing size.
  border 1px solid transparent
  border-radius $button_border_radius
  outline none
  cursor pointer
  transition background 120ms ease, border-color 120ms ease, color 120ms ease, box-shadow 120ms ease

  // The press is a deeper fill, not a lift.
  &:active:not(:disabled)
  &:active:not([disabled])
    background rgba(23, 24, 26, 0.12)
    box-shadow none

  // THE HOVER IS THE MENU SECTIONS' LIFT, shared from variables.styl rather
  // than reproduced here. Gated on a fine pointer for the reason every hover
  // state should be: a touch device has no un-hover, so the lift would stick to
  // the last button tapped until something else was.
  @media (pointer: fine)
    &:hover:not(:disabled)
    &:hover:not([disabled])
      hover_lift()
      border-color $prose_rule_strong

  // The ring is the focus indicator rather than a colour swap alone: a border
  // that only changes hue is invisible to anyone who cannot separate the two.
  &:focus-visible
    border-color $prose_ink
    box-shadow 0 0 0 3px rgba(23, 24, 26, 0.08)

  &:disabled
  &[disabled]
    opacity 0.45
    cursor default
    user-select none

  + .button
    margin-left 8px

  .count
    position absolute
    top 3px
    right 5px
    margin 0
    padding 3px
    font-family $prose_mono
    font-size 8px
    line-height 5px
    border 0
    border-radius 99em
    background rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.6)

  // The quiet one in a pair, and the only variant with no fill and no lift. A
  // text button that carried the light ground would be a filled button with a
  // different name, and one that lifted on hover would be a filled button that
  // waited. It sits inside table rows, where a hard offset shadow under a
  // one-word action reads as the row itself moving. The ink is the whole
  // response, so both the base fill and the base lift are undone here.
  // An icon button is the same argument as a text button with a glyph in place
  // of the word, so it takes the same treatment: no fill, no lift, and the ink
  // is the whole response.
  &.button__text
  &.button__icon
    color $prose_muted
    background transparent
    border-color transparent

    @media (pointer: fine)
      &:hover:not(:disabled)
      &:hover:not([disabled])
        color $prose_ink
        background transparent
        border-color transparent
        box-shadow none

    // THE FOCUS RING HAS TO BE RESTATED HERE, AND LAST. `border-color:
    // transparent` above sits at the SAME specificity as the `:focus-visible`
    // rule further up the file and comes after it, so it won — every text and
    // icon button had no visible focus indicator at all, with `outline: none`
    // already suppressing the UA ring and only a 1.18:1 box-shadow left. Seven
    // icon buttons carried MUI's focus treatment until they were converted, so
    // this is the ring they are owed rather than a new affordance.
    //
    // `:not(:disabled)` and the position after the hover block are both load
    // bearing: the hover rule above also clears border-color and sits a step
    // higher, so a plain `&:focus-visible` would go dark again the moment the
    // pointer crossed a button the keyboard had already reached.
    &:focus-visible:not(:disabled)
      border-color $prose_ink

  &.active
  &.button__active
    color $accentColor
    border-color $accentColor

  &.button__small
    height 26px
    padding 0 10px
    font-size 12px

    // THE GLYPH TRACKS THE BUTTON. A 24px icon in a 26px control leaves one
    // pixel either side, which is why every MUI call site that set
    // `size='small'` also had to remember `<Icon small />` — two props for one
    // decision, and three sites got the pair wrong in one direction or the
    // other. Sizing from the button's own class makes the coupling structural,
    // so `small` is now the whole statement.
    .icon
      width 18px
      height 18px

// A GROUP'S CHILD RULES LIVE HERE, NOT IN button-group.styl, and the reason is
// specificity rather than taste. The base selector above carries a four-:not()
// guard chain, so it lands at 0,5,0 — an override written in button-group.styl
// under its own class would sit at 0,3,0 and lose to it SILENTLY, which is this
// repo's characteristic CSS failure. Written here, `&` expands to the whole
// chain and the override cannot be quietly outranked. These are also the rules
// that undo `+ .button { margin-left: 8px }` a few lines up, so keeping them
// next to it is what makes the pair readable.
.button-group > .button:not(.MuiButton-root):not(.MuiIconButton-root):not(.MuiFab-root):not(.MuiButtonBase-root)
  // Segments share an edge: the pair spacing is undone and the 1px borders
  // overlap, so two adjacent buttons draw one rule between them rather than two.
  + .button
    margin-left -1px
    border-left-color $prose_rule_strong

  // THE LIFT BELONGS TO THE GROUP, NOT TO A SEGMENT. `hover_lift()` on the base
  // rule gives each button its own `$box_shadow` — a hard 4px offset with no
  // blur — so hovering the middle of the auction's `- / Bid $N / +` stepper
  // dropped a shadow INSIDE the control, printed across the `+` segment abutting
  // it, and the segment read as one button floating off a control it is welded
  // to. Measured: hovering `Bid $1` computed `rgb(208,208,208) 4px 4px 0 0` on
  // that segment alone while the group itself computed `none`.
  //
  // The shadow moves to `.button-group:hover` in button-group.styl; the
  // segment keeps the ground and border half of the lift, because WHICH segment
  // the pointer is on is still the thing a stepper has to answer.
  //
  // This rule outranks the base hover it undoes — 0,6,0 against 0,5,0 — which
  // is the same specificity argument the block header makes, and it has to,
  // since `box-shadow none` written at a lower rank would emit and never apply.
  @media (pointer: fine)
    &:hover:not(:disabled)
    &:hover:not([disabled])
      box-shadow none

  &:not(:first-child)
    border-top-left-radius 0
    border-bottom-left-radius 0

  &:not(:last-child)
    border-top-right-radius 0
    border-bottom-right-radius 0
